    <example number="2.26">
      <problem>
        <p>Andreas bought a used car for $12,000. Because the car was 4-years old, its price was $$\frac{3}{4}$$ of the original price, when the car was new. What was the original price of the car?</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="This table demonstrates the step-by-step process for solving a word problem involving fractions, from identifying the unknown to checking the solution." number="Table 2.4">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>What are you asked to find?</cell>
              <cell>The original price of the car</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Assign a variable.</cell>
              <cell>Let $$p$$ = the original price.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Write a sentence that gives the information to find it.</cell>
              <cell>$12,000 is $$\frac{3}{4}$$ of the original price.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Translate into an equation.</cell>
              <cell>$$12,000 = \frac{3}{4}p$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Solve.</cell>
              <cell>$$\begin{matrix} {\frac{4}{3}(12,000) = \frac{4}{3} \cdot \frac{3}{4}p} \\ {16,000 = p} \end{matrix}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>The original cost of the car was $16,000.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Check: Is $$\frac{3}{4}$$ of $16,000 equal to $12,000?
$$\begin{array}{rll} {\frac{3}{4} \cdot 16,000} &amp; \overset{?}{=} &amp; 12,000 \\ 12,000 &amp; = &amp; {12,000✓} \end{array}$$</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
